.TH "SERIALPORT.READABLE" "3JS" "May 19, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
SerialPort.readable \- SerialPort: readable property
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]Secure context:\f[R] This feature is available only in secure
contexts (HTTPS).
.PP
\f[B]Note:\f[R] This feature is available in Dedicated Web Workers.
.PP
The \f[B]\f[CB]readable\f[B]\f[R] read\-only property of the
\f[CR]SerialPort\f[R] interface returns a \f[CR]ReadableStream\f[R] for
receiving data from the device connected to the port.
Chunks read from this stream are instances of \f[CR]Uint8Array\f[R].
This property is non\-null as long as the port is open and has not
encountered a fatal error.
.SH VALUE
A \f[CR]ReadableStream\f[R].
.SH EXAMPLES
The following example shows how to read data from a port.
The outer loop handles non\-fatal errors, creating a new reader until a
fatal error is encountered and \f[CR]readable\f[R] becomes
\f[CR]null\f[R].
.IP
.EX
\f[B]while\f[R] (port.readable) {
  \f[B]const\f[R] reader = port.readable.getReader();
  \f[B]try\f[R] {
    \f[B]while\f[R] (\f[B]true\f[R]) {
      \f[B]const\f[R] { value, done } = \f[B]await\f[R] reader.read();
      \f[B]if\f[R] (done) {
        \f[I]// |reader| has been canceled.\f[R]
        \f[B]break\f[R];
      }
      \f[I]// Do something with |value|\&...\f[R]
    }
  } \f[B]catch\f[R] (error) {
    \f[I]// Handle |error|\&...\f[R]
  } \f[B]finally\f[R] {
    reader.releaseLock();
  }
}
.EE
